One transcript per compiler stage, in pipeline order, so the whole
catalogue of things a user can hit sits in one readable file. Every one
of them exits 1 and writes to stderr; nothing is written to disk.

A lexer error, reported by the default (token-dump) mode. Note the
`path:line:col` prefix with no space after the path:

```console
$ hllc unterminated-string.hll
? 1
1:1 Ident "service"
1:9 Ident "web"
1:13 LBrace "{"
2:3 Ident "image"
unterminated-string.hll:2:9: unterminated string literal

```

The same file through `--parse` prints the same error, except that this
path puts a space after the path. The inconsistency is real and worth
having recorded rather than hidden — `--parse` prefixes the whole
rendered error, which already begins with its own `line:col`.

```console
$ hllc --parse unterminated-string.hll
? 1
unterminated-string.hll: 2:9: unterminated string literal

```

A parser error:

```console
$ hllc --parse missing-value.hll
? 1
missing-value.hll: 3:1: expected a value or `{`, found `}`

```

A composition error: `with` naming a template that does not exist.

```console
$ hllc --build unknown-template.hll
? 1
unknown-template.hll:3:8: unknown template `hardened`

```

A link error: a `use` whose target is not there. The message names the
file that could not be opened, which is the imported path rather than
the one on the command line.

```console
$ hllc --build missing-import.hll
? 1
shared/templates.hll: No such file or directory (os error 2)

```

A codegen error: an interpolation with no matching source.

```console
$ hllc --build bad-interpolation.hll
? 1
bad-interpolation.hll:3:18: unknown interpolation `{{nmae}}`

```

And the plainest failure of all, an input file that does not exist:

```console
$ hllc --build nonexistent.hll
? 1
nonexistent.hll: No such file or directory (os error 2)

```
